{
  // Dudo-Core — the TYPE-NEGATIVE probe's compiler configuration.
  //
  // OWNER: qa-agent. `packages/testing/**` is qa-agent's territory
  // (docs/architecture/boundaries.md). THIS FILE CHANGES NO ROOT CONFIGURATION and adds no
  // dependency.
  //
  // ===========================================================================================
  // IT `extends` THE ROOT RATHER THAN COPYING IT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN
  // ===========================================================================================
  //
  // A type-negative probe asserts that certain code DOES NOT COMPILE. That claim is only
  // meaningful with respect to a particular language configuration: `{ principalId: 'x' }` is
  // rejected as a `MembershipAdmission` under `strict`, and a probe running with different
  // options would be asserting a fact about a language Dudo does not ship.
  //
  // So every compiler option comes from the root by inheritance. If the Team Lead turns on
  // `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, or changes `strict`, or relaxes `verbatimModuleSyntax`, this
  // probe follows automatically. A copied block would drift, and it would drift SILENTLY —
  // the probe would keep passing while checking a stricter or looser language than the product.
  //
  // ===========================================================================================
  // ONLY `include` AND `exclude` ARE OVERRIDDEN, AND BOTH HAVE TO BE
  // ===========================================================================================
  //
  // The root `exclude`s `packages/testing` — deliberately, with its reason recorded there: that
  // tree is Node code and the root config targets a Worker. `exclude` is INHERITED by `extends`,
  // so without the override below this project would resolve to zero files and `tsc` would exit
  // 0 having checked nothing. That is the exact shape of a probe that passes because it never
  // ran, so it is called out here rather than left as a silent trap.
  //
  // `include` IS `cases` ONLY. The runner (`../run-type-negative.ts`) and the rest of
  // `packages/testing` import `node:fs`, `node:sqlite` and `node:child_process`, and the root
  // sets `"types": []` with no `@types/node` installed. Pulling them in would produce dozens of
  // "Cannot find module 'node:fs'" diagnostics that have nothing to do with any assertion — and
  // the runner treats an unexpected diagnostic as a failure, correctly. The fixtures under
  // `cases/` import from `platform/core` only and touch no Node API, so they need nothing the
  // root does not already provide.
  "extends": "../../../tsconfig.json",
  "include": ["cases"],
  "exclude": []
}
